Crohn's Disease (Mild Ileocecal)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Intermittent right lower quadrant twinges, mild loose stools, stable weight

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-011
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of right lower quadrant abdominal pain requiring exclusion of acute appendicitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, localizing right iliac fossa pain. Immediate clinical differentiation from acute appendicitis or viscus perforation was strictly required to determine the need for emergency surgical admission prior to the next in-hours period, preventing catastrophic rupture.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: There is a sharp, grabbing pain in my lower right side that keeps catching me, and I'm terrified my appendix is bursting.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute flare of low-grade terminal ileum inflammation that commenced 8 hours ago. Symptoms began as a diffuse central abdominal ache before migrating and localizing to the right lower quadrant as sharp, intermittent cramps. Patient reports two episodes of unformed bowel motions this evening. Denies anorexia, intractable vomiting, or rigors.
Functional Status: Independent, currently lying still in fetal position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, exhibiting mild guarding but easily distractible.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized mild tenderness to deep palpation in the right iliac fossa. Intermittent right lower quadrant twinges, mild loose stools, stable weight. McBurney's point tenderness is equivocal, but rebound tenderness and Rovsing's sign are completely negative. Bowel sounds are active. No palpable abdominal masses.
Environmental Observations: Tidy residential environment, easy access to bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent paracetamol use, no current immunomodulators.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Crohn's Disease Flare (Mild Ileocecal) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of severe peritonitis, negative rebound tenderness, and absence of classic anorexia or high fever.

Renal Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe colicky loin-to-groin radiation, lack of extreme restlessness, and negative renal angle percussion tenderness.

Mesenteric Adenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of preceding viral upper respiratory symptoms and prominent localized ileocecal features correlating with known autoimmune history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, migrating right lower quadrant pain, satisfying the classic prodromal timeline for acute appendicitis. Triage correctly prioritized a physical exam to exclude an acute surgical abdomen. Detailed palpation confirmed localized ileal inflammation characteristic of mild Crohn's disease, firmly ruling out acute appendicitis or peritoneal irritation. This bedside diagnostic differentiation prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission and computed tomography scan, safely allowing outpatient symptomatic management that could not be deferred until the next morning due to the risk of imminent perforation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral simple analgesia and an antispasmodic. Arranged urgent outpatient gastroenterology review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a warm compress to the right lower quadrant for 20 minutes whenever the sharp twinges occur, focusing on slow, deep diaphragmatic breathing. Actively relaxing the abdominal wall will decrease visceral hypersensitivity, providing you with immediate, self-managed pain control.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours for calprotectin and CRP testing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the pain becomes constant and severe, if you develop a fever over 38.0 degrees Celsius, or if you begin vomiting uncontrollably.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal inflammatory markers and endoscopy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Mild terminal ileitis causes transient localized spasms that mimic appendicitis but resolve with gut rest and targeted therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if the pain worsens dramatically when you move or cough, or if your abdomen becomes completely rigid.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is caused by localized inflammation in the last segment of your small bowel, leading to muscle spasms. It is not an infected appendix.
Actionable Steps: Rest the bowel by consuming only clear fluids and easily digestible foods for the next 24 hours. Utilize the warm compress for pain relief.

